abstract: >-
  Virtual-cell models are increasingly evaluated at finer biological resolution,
  yet benchmarks rarely test whether measurements reproducibly resolve the scored
  distinctions. We introduce PertResolve, a diagnostic framework that separates
  perturbation detection, identification and response prediction, and compares
  model performance with a matched split-half reproducibility reference. Across
  470 coding-variant conditions of TP53, KRAS, GATA1 and JAK1 (321,043 cells),
  feature-based predictors recovered transcriptional direction, as did a
  gene-mean baseline without allele information, but neither they nor released
  perturbation models reliably identified alleles. Split-half references revealed
  distinct limitations behind similar model scores: TP53 and KRAS were
  measurement-limited, whereas JAK1 was model-limited, its reference exceeding
  the best feature-based predictor by 0.36 in perturbation discrimination score.
  Across 31 configurations from 14 public resources, detection depended on
  perturbation signal relative to within-condition variation, whereas
  identification depended on separation from nearby competitors. PertResolve
  indicates whether a prediction task needs better measurements or better
  models, and guides benchmark design.
